"Prospect Park" was a neighborhood park located in the Lower East Side of New York City, NY. It was created by the city government in 1897 to provide residents with recreational opportunities and green space.

The park was designed by architect Charles H. Eames and completed in 1902. The park featured a variety of playground equipment, including swings, slides, and seesaws. It also included a baseball field, basketball court, and a small pond.

Prospect Park was one of the first parks in the city to be equipped with electric street lighting, which made it easier for residents to navigate the neighborhood at night. The park was also home to several local organizations, including the YMCA, the American Red Cross, and the Salvation Army.

Despite its name, Prospect Park was not a public park, but rather an experimental facility used primarily by residents of the Lower East Side. In recent years, the park has been converted into a mixed-use development, featuring apartments, retail spaces, and community centers. Despite these changes, Prospect Park remains an important part of the Lower East Side's cultural landscape.
